---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { components, SNAPSHOT_DATE } from '../data/components';
import { compileComponentStatus } from '../data/live-status';

const order: Record<string, number> = { published: 0, 'in development': 1, planned: 2 };
const sorted = (await Promise.all(components.map(compileComponentStatus))).sort(
  (a, b) => order[a.status] - order[b.status],
);
const compiledCount = sorted.filter((c) => c.stage).length;
const builtOn = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
---

<Base
  title="Roadmap — Pegma"
  description="Where every Pegma component stands, what gates what, and what 1.0 requires."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Roadmap</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Pegma ships component by component, each through its own phased plan, each gated by real
      consumers rather than dates. This page is the aggregate view.
    </p>
    <p class="notice">
      Status lines below are compiled at build time ({builtOn}) from each repository's public
      <code>docs/PROJECT_PLAN.md</code> — {compiledCount} of {components.length} components carry a
      plan; the rest (and any fetch miss) fall back to a hand-written snapshot dated
      {SNAPSHOT_DATE}. The repos win any disagreement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>Where things stand</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Component</th>
          <th>Status</th>
          <th>Now</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          sorted.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <a href={`/stack#${c.repo}`}>{c.title}</a>
              </td>
              <td>
                <span class="status" data-status={c.status}>
                  {c.status}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td>{c.stage ?? c.now}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What gates what</h2>
  <p>
    The dependencies that actually pace the work — none of them are dates:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Authorization Core → its consumers.</strong> Its public <code>0.x</code> packages
      now exist, and this reference application composes the Identity claims adapter with
      first-party Identity and Sessions. Broader consumer migrations still determine when those
      contracts are stable enough for <code>1.0</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Webhooks is evidence-gated.</strong> The ledger and RetireGolden consumer migration
      are implemented, but Phase 2 remains operationally open until production Stripe traffic is
      observed through the package shape. Phase 3 waits for a second real, non-Stripe provider
      rather than inventing a synthetic consumer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consumers pull components into existence.</strong> Support Desk supplied the first
      durable mail consumer; Identity supplied the second, triggering Mail's extraction and public
      release. Identity also pulled Rate Limit into production composition instead of leaving it
      as a speculative utility.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>This site is a gate of its own.</strong> pegma.dev deploys on Cloudflare and now runs
      a real Identity consumer over the Cloudflare D1 adapter, which passes the same conformance
      suite as the Azure adapter. Two clouds, one suite — that is the portability claim, tested.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What 1.0 means</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing reaches <code>1.0</code> on ambition. The ecosystem rule is that breaking changes stay
    permitted until multiple real consumers complete adoption — a component earns stability by
    being used, not by being finished. Until then, every package is honestly <code>0.x</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
